    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_276dc49bd5b82584192c6f6d6874cc6fe6a07feed258689f323cb2ab25a1e5d5",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Taking a two-eigenvalue collision as a close-pair limit changes the law of the remaining eigenvalues to a lower-dimensional Haar ensemble tilted by the fourth power of the characteristic polynomial at the collision point. This tilted law supplies the remaining-root observables that enter the first correction to the quadratic critical-point displacement: finite-N polynomial coefficients are averaged through characteristic-polynomial ratios, while the corresponding microscopic conditioned density is obtained through an orthogonal-polynomial kernel and is used in the conditional zeta calculation.",
